A||||16||fiero56||Rod bearing replacement||||1||
Z||000000||fiero56||08-15-2000||11:36 AM||||I have '88 Formula(v-6) with just over 160,000 miles. The engine is unrebuilt, burns no oil, gets great gas mileage, shows no signs of anything wrong with it. About 5,000 miles ago, I began to hear some knocking coming from the bottom end under low rpm load(1,200rpm and under). The knocking can only be heard when I am beside a parked car, a garage wall, something like that beside me on the passenger side with the passenger window down. <BR>I put 10w40 in place of the normal 5w30, and it helped some, but the knocking is still there under low rpm load. I am pretty sure this is cause by at least one of the rod bearings being worn.<BR>I don't have plans to keep this engine in the car much longer(1 year at most). If I were to just replace the rod bearings without pulling the crank or having any machine work done, how long can I expect this fix to last? I don't want to let this get much worse.||||reg||1
Z||000001||1FST2M6||08-15-2000||12:20 PM||||jack the car up drain the oil.. drop the oil pan and pull each rod cap by itself making sure you don't turn it around push the rod side bearing out and check the crank for scratches... replace the old bearing with new either stock size or what i did .001+ replace the bearing on the rod with assembly lube on the crank face of the bearing.. making sure the rod and cap side are clean. Torque the rod bolts to their factory specs then rotate the crank one rev. to make sure there is no binding. and do this too all 6.. #5 went bad on my SE and scratched the crank a bit i got some 400grit emory cloth (just easily bendable sandpaper) and filed the entire radius of the crank to try and get a uniform scratch free surface. on te 65-86 cars teh oil pan is a PAIN IN THE butt.. the 2 bolts under the pulleys suck to get off.. the 87-88 cars have a flat pan and aren't that bad... just make sure whe you put it back together the surfaces are free from ANY grit... took me about 5 hours from start to finish... only do one at a time. if you have spun a bearing you'll need a strong pick to get the bearing guides out of their place on the rod or cap.. there are little extrusions that hold the bearing in place.. if they don't fit in the hole you've wasted your time and it'll either bind up and spin again or bind up and kill it's self and take the block with it.. the bearings do take a bit of pressure to seat but not much... good luck getting the oil pan to not leak.. you have to have "the touch" to make the cork gasket actually work.. i don't so i had a shop employee do it for me... NO SILICONE!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> you can use silicone... it's just messy.  Good luck.. it's really not that hard.. just time consumming<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 08-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||08-15-2000||03:38 PM||||I was out again today, and put the windows down. I can hear the knocking slightly even at idle at an intersection without anything beside me to bounce the noise into the cabin. Leaking oil pan isn't a problem, mine already does <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. This is something I can do in the driveway without too much hassle then. I don't think I spun a bearing, I think its just age, oil pressure is mainly determined by bearings, and mine has been dropping over the last year. I can't wait until that 3.4 goes in. <BR>I assume that since no machine work will be done, that these bearings won't last quite as long, is this correct? Also, any particular brand you recommend?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||08-15-2000||08:37 PM||||I think whatever manufacturer you go with is going to be OK, especially for a year. The only thing I would do differently from the above description, is I would mic the journals in several places to verify the radius is nice and round, that the face is flat (no low spots runing the radius) and there's no taper.<BR>Also, to start, I wouldn't go oversize on the bearings (unless that's what's installed now) and I would Plasti-Gage the bearings, and only install oversize bearings if they're needed. My opinion is that the clearances are too close to be fooling around guessing if the clearance is aceptable.<BR>Good luck!<P>-Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||08-15-2000||11:00 PM||||I'll be out tomorrow morning trying to find a set. Are they all made out of the same material, or are there different types like brake pads?||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||08-16-2000||08:58 AM||||Just got back from AutoZone. Bought stock size bearings by Clevite. I'll let the engine cool a bit, and then get to work. <P>What type of lube should I put on the bearings before I install them? What is assembly lube? Do you guys recommend changing the oil before 3000 miles since I am replacing these bearings? Any break-in procedures for them?||||reg||1
Z||000006||1FST2M6||08-16-2000||09:33 AM||||be nice to it till you know it works.. assembly lube is just a thick oil thats kinda like anti-seize for threads. DON'T USE ANTI-SEIZE! thats bad for moving parts.. good idea on the plasti-guage.. also micing it is a good idea if you can read a mic and understand how to fix it if your crank is out-of-round. I tosed in .001 cause i figured there was wear on the crank from 97k of spinnin and i know a few thou on nitrous. it was ok for a while till it got to SD then started knocking again... (well missouri actually) but Its still being driven... the oil was change to 20-50 and an additive (CD-2)was tossed in.. YES change your oil you'll have to drain it anyway since your there might as well... ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Taijiguy||08-16-2000||10:54 AM||||If you PlastiGage the bearings, make sure you do it with them DRY! NO LUBE! And for SURE, don't turn the crank while the bearing is installed dry! Then scratch off the plastiGage with your fingernail and a rag, and then lube everything up real nice.<P>The most important aspect of assembly lube is that it's oil soluble. Not all grease is oil soluble, which means it doesn't wash out completely, and can cause problems later. AutoZone has assembly lube, but it's behind the counter so you have to ask for it. (at least at the one I go to it was)<P>Good luck with everything!<P>-Taiji<p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 08-16-2000).]||||reg||4
Z||000008||fiero56||08-17-2000||02:57 AM||||Update:<P>I spent most of today trying to get that oil pan off. Maybe the '88 is different because of the different cradle, different engine mounting, etc, but it is impossible to take off the oil pan with the engine sitting in stock location. It hits the flywheel, the cradle, the exhaust, and the engine mount. NO WAY is that thing coming off. I spent most of the afternoon reinstalling all the bolts, two are still on the garage floor. Guess a 3.4 is closer in my future than I thought. I just can't stand driving around with a rod knocking.||||reg||9
Z||000009||fiero56||08-17-2000||01:01 PM||||I can't get the last damn bolt in the &lt;insert many expletives here&gt; oil pan!!! &lt;more expletives&gt; This is the bolt that attaches to the pan above the engine mount. I have tried everything in my power, and wasted more than 6 hours of my time trying to get this damn pan back ON the engine, even though it never came off. What really pisses me, is I have spent more than a day on this damn engine, and haven't accomplished a damn thing!!! &lt;many more expletives&gt; I should have just left this piece of &lt;expletive&gt; alone! <BR>Temper has gotten the best of me. About 20 minutes ago when I thought I almost had the bolt threading in, it fell out. I resorted to throwing anything within reach at the car(two rathcets, many sockets, some nuts and bolts, rocks, etc). Maybe I should just push the car off the stands without putting the wheels back on! I am going to have to lift this f#$%^$ engine OFF the cradle to get the damn bolt in. $# ^&$*&#@!#$^&||||reg||8
Z||000010||1FST2M6||08-17-2000||01:24 PM||||my 86s bolts were a pain too.. Ed just had to walk away for a few minutes... I got it all off with no problem... and all the bearings changed, but it was a pain puting it back on... uum i thought there was more clearance on the 88 than the 85-86 cause the oil pan has a flat flange where it mounts.. (looks that way on my new 87GT) huummm sorry.. don't have any good ideas... tighen what you have and silicon the piss out of the part with out bolts..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> anyone else have any ideas.. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Standard||08-17-2000||09:21 PM||||Gumption trap!  Read Zen and the Art of Motorcycle Maintanence.  Lots of good ideas in there that will help when you're working on cars like these...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  walk away and try it again when you're calmed down.<P>------------------<BR>Mike -- Golden Valley, MN<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 149,000 miles and counting!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif"> ||||reg||4
Z||000012||2birds||08-17-2000||10:35 PM||||You should see how my clutch job is going.  Wasted a whole day because of a frozen caliper bleeder screw.<P>I didn't think you could get the pan off without dropping the cradle, either.<P>Are you sure the knock you hear isn't just a loose catalytic converter brick?  Mine sounded just like rod knock/piston slap.  New cat = no knocking for me.<p>[This message has been edited by 2birds (edited 08-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||fiero56||08-17-2000||10:50 PM||||Pretty sure its bearings. When the engine is cold(oil thicker)there is no knocking. Once the engine is warmed up(oil thinner)it starts knocking. RPMs over 1,500 = no knocking. I wish it were the CAT. I have walked away from this job several times. I keep coming back and have the same problem. <P>This is my daily driver( have to keep bumming rides from everyone until I get the oil pan back on), and I can't go without it very long. I might have to drop the cradle to get the pan back on. What a waste of time. I'll crawl under there with my camera tommorrow and post pictures where the oil pan is blocked because of: the fluwheel, the cradle, the exhaust, and the engine mount. I can't believe I didn't notice 4 things blocking removal of the pan when I started. ||||reg||1
Z||000014||GT Bastard||08-18-2000||09:59 PM||||I'm dealing with the exact same problem, Fiero56.... The motor it in the works, so I'm hoping that it's out of the machine shop before it blows.  The first thing I did when I got the car was switch to 20w50 valvoline race oil and some CD2(like 1FST mentioned.)  It quieted down a noticeably and I've got about 300 miles on it with that nasty knock.  I've event gotten brave a few times and raced it up to 5000 rpm.  I'm keeping my fingers crossed.  ||||reg||1
Z||000015||88formula||08-20-2000||10:19 AM||||Don't forget to replace your oil pump. Replacing the oil pump is very important with 160,000 miles on it.<P>My GM manual says to remove the flywheel shield, starter, oil pan bolts, and oil pan for an 88 formula.||||reg||1
Z||000016||RedOktober||08-26-2000||03:12 AM||||Boy, I'm I glad I'm not the only one who has had a problem here.<BR>Low oil pressure, Just 2 lines above the red warning bars. Decided to change the oil pump with a high pressure one and do the bearings while it was all opened.<BR>Everything went smoothly, even plastigauged it too. just a little taper, but within specs.<P>Started it up and was smiling.. til the oil warmed up...oil pressure started to drop.<P>Now upgrading to either a 3.1/2 or a 3.4<P>If you are having problems... this is not a recommended route. Get it fixed properly.<BR>PS... Took me and Mr.Coffee 7 hours and she drove for 10 miles. ||||reg||1
